for decades large companies have been  trying to dominate the online video  calling and messaging market we've seen  everything from webex msn messenger and  skype to google hangouts and zoom but it  seems like people's favorite doesn't  come from any of these large companies  rather their top choice is often discord  this is quite ironic given that projects  like google hangouts and skype have  billions of dollars behind them and  thousands of employees  after all microsoft literally spent 8.5  billion dollars just acquire skype so  clearly they take this sector quite  seriously yet they're getting crushed by  a relatively small startup that was  founded by a gamer skype does  technically have more active users  currently clocking in at 300 million  while discord only has about half of  that at 150 million but when you  consider that skype was launched in 2003  and that discord wasn't launched till  2015 it becomes clear who is really  winning the user race not to mention a  lot of skype's user base comes from  companies forcing their employees to use  skype while virtually everyone who uses  discord chooses it out of free will so  here's how a gamer showed up big tech in  one of the most saturated industries in  the world  taking a look back the story of discord  dates back to a man named jason siptron  who was born on september 21 1984 in  florida jason's interest in technology  and especially gaming started very early  on in life thanks to his father and  childhood friends  apparently jason's father was a massive  fan of technology himself and he was  always tinkering with personal computers  in his free time at the time personal  computers weren't actually all that  useful to ordinary people given that  their capabilities were extremely  limited and windows 3.1 hadn't even come  out yet nonetheless being a businessman  jason's father could see that pcs were  gonna become huge hence the constant  tinkering with pcs while jason's  father's curiosity no doubt rubbed off  on jason jason says that a lot of his  passion for tech comes from gaming and  it was actually a childhood friend that  introduced him to games apparently one  of his childhood friends owned an nes  and jason had the chance to play super  mario bros on it jason described this  experience as quote the most amazing  thing i'd ever seen in my entire life as  he grew older his love for gaming  evolved from being a player to being a  developer jason took his first computer  science class in middle school and  became friends with another coding  enthusiast within class soon after jason  hosted a sleepover for his 13th birthday  and once everyone else went to sleep  jason and his comp side friends spent  the night trying to code a role-playing  game using qbasic given that this was  their first try the game probably wasn't  that great however jason says that this  experience made it clear to him that his  career had to be related to gaming  moving forward throughout the rest of  his teenage years jason continued to be  an avid gamer some of his favorite games  include super metroid chrono trigger  earthbound uniracers street fighter  mortal kombat and most importantly a  final fantasy vi aside from gaming jason  stark uses knowledge of computers to  start earning income as a teenager jason  says that he quickly realized that most  people were relatively clueless about  computers which gave him an opportunity  to help them fix computer problems and  earn some money jason's father was  especially supportive of this endeavor  and he would regularly drive jason all  around the neighborhood so that he could  make his side hustle a reality as jason  slowly built up a reputation as a  computer prodigy people started to ask  him to do bigger tasks such as writing  code for them in fact the side hustle  grew so big that jason's parents helped  him set up a bank account and jason  started receiving wire transfers from  customers if your customers were using  wire transfers to pay you you were  clearly making big money despite this  jason didn't instantly delve into  starting a full-on development company  he says that he was intimidated of  hiring people so he preferred to keep  his side hustle a solo adventure but  with jason's ambitions it was just a  matter of time until his projects  outgrew him  moving into college jason attended for  sale university in florida presumably to  major in computer science given that  jason had been coding for several years  at this point college wasn't that  difficult for him and he would end up  graduating early after graduation jason  moved to san rafael california and  picked up a job as a console game  developer initially he got himself his  own place and lived alone but he says  that this got boring pretty quickly so  he moved into a college house and became  roommates with nine uc berkeley students  while this may seem suboptimal at first  glance this actually turned out to be  extremely valuable you see about six  months after moving into the house a man  named rohan relin moved in like jason  rohan was a software enthusiast and his  uncle and he were actually planning on  launching an incubator called uweb that  was supposed to help mobile games get  off the ground as you would guess jason  was extremely intrigued by this idea and  rohan would eventually invite jason to  join the incubator at the incubator  jason would meet another developer named  danielle casley and together they would  develop their first major mobile game  called aurora faint the game combined  two of jason's favorite genres which  were role-playing games and puzzle games  so jason was basically living his dream  of being able to work on developing a  game that really interested him as a  career  all of this took place in late 2007 and  early 2008 so apple had just released  the iphone and the app store wasn't even  a thing yet jason and danielle weren't  exactly developing the game specifically  for the iphone but once the app store  was launched it was clear to them that  they had to address the iphone market  this was actually perfect timing because  it allowed aurora fane to be one of the  first games on the app store and the  given that most of the original apps on  the app store weren't that great aurora  faint was quickly able to build up a  solid user base of over 1 million users  despite this the game wasn't very  profitable given that there was no  recurring revenue from customers apple  didn't even support in-app purchases at  the time so jason and daniella only  earned money on the upfront cost of the  game which was only one dollar at one  point jason only had two months worth of  funding left and he remembers calling  his parents and telling them that he  might need to move in with them but  luckily for jason that's when he would  come up with his best idea yet  jason's breakthrough moment came when he  was having a conversation with rohan's  uncle peter about where to take aurora  faint during this conversation jason  said that it was weird that xbox live  wasn't available on iphone given how  fast the mobile gaming market was  growing it you would think that either  microsoft or apple would have released  something similar to xbox live but this  wasn't the case and this is when rawan's  uncle realized that aurora faint already  had aspects of xbox live within it so it  wouldn't be that difficult to create  their own version of xbox live called  open faint jason didn't have any money  left though so he pitched this idea to a  few investors who agreed to fund the app  if he could prove that game developers  would support the endeavor later that  week jason posted the idea of open find  on techcrunch which resulted in hundreds  of game developers signing up and  pledging their support and with that  jason was funded and he would code like  crazy over the next several weeks and  eventually on february 17 2009 jason  would launch open faint given that there  was no native alternative and the  overwhelming support from game  developers open faint quickly caught on  amongst mobile gamers open fan's user  base grew into the tens of millions  which allowed jason to complete funding  rounds that each raised millions it  looked like things couldn't get any  better and that's exactly when apple  launched game center jason says that he  felt like the heir was being sucked out  of his lungs as he realized that he was  now competing against apple despite  jason's fears though game center  actually ended up being a massive  propellant for open paint you see here  jason integrated open feint into game  center and integrated game center into  open faint so open find users could  enjoy the benefits of game center and  the new game center users could  naturally discover openfint it was a  win-win scenario and at the peak of open  paint success they boasted 120 million  users and supported 7 000 games given  this popularity i don't think you'd be  too surprised here that gree would go on  to buy open faint for 104 million  dollars it's not clear what percentage  of open paint jason owned before the  acquisition but i think it's safe to  assume that the sale made jason a  multi-millionaire at the very least if  not a deca millionaire well jason had a  pretty nice ending the same cannot be  said about open faint around the same  time a coalition of gamers would sue  open faint for violating their privacy  and a little over a year later gre would  announce that they're shutting down open  paint due to overwhelming backlash and  that was the better end of open faint  while losing open faint was likely a  massive blow for jason and he was pretty  well off financially so he had the  luxury of taking some time off and  focusing on something else initially  jason actually turned to creating  another gaming company called hammer and  chisel in 2012. this company would  release a mobile game called fates  forever but this never really garnered  much attention disappointed by the  launch jason often spent a lot of his  time playing games like final fantasy  xiv and the league of legends one of the  first things that stuck out to him as he  played these games was that their  in-game voice communication services  were absolute garbage so jason set out  to develop a user-friendly voice  communication service that had minimal  impact on game performance and that's  how discord was founded over the next  year jason worked with euweb to develop  discord and given that he'd been a gamer  for his entire life he knew exactly what  gamers wanted jason would go on to  launch discord in may of 2015 and he  says that he didn't market it to any  specific audience but naturally one of  the first communities that started  embracing the platform were gaming  subreddits this quickly made it popular  among twitch streamers and eventually  esports and land tournament gamers one  of the first companies that saw the  potential of discord was time warner  cable who invested into the company in  late 2016. two years later microsoft  would also embrace the company by  bringing discord support to xbox live  users microsoft would actually  eventually try to buy discord for 12  billion dollars but jason would turn  down this offer in april of 2021 and  given that discord has since  successfully completed a funding round  at a 15 billion valuation it looks like  turning down the acquisition deal was  the right decision but it's not all  sunshines and rainbows ahead for discord  given that discord doesn't directly  charge any of its users the revenue per  user is likely not that great and  considering how they're constantly  completing new funding rounds it's  likely that discord is losing money this  is fine for a startup but you can only  keep up a money losing company for so  long so discord needs to focus on  monetization and profitability asap but  i think jason is well aware of that and  that's exactly what he's trying to do  so over the next couple of years it's  likely that discord only goes even  bigger and potentially even becomes the  world's largest voice communication  platform but we'll just have to wait and  see what's your favorite aspect about  discord comment that down below  also drop a like if you think that  discord blows out the competition and of  course consider checking out our  international channels to watch your  videos in other languages and consider  subscribing to see more questions  logically answered but until then i'm  hurry and i'll see you guys on the next  one

